# DBTITLE 1,Synthetic Data — {{DOMAIN_NAME}}
# Generates realistic, domain-specific sample data for all tables using dbldatagen.
# This template is DOMAIN-AGNOSTIC — all semantic intelligence (values, distributions)
# is injected by the LLM at notebook generation time from synthetic_data_spec.yaml.
#
# Architecture: UTILITIES-ONLY scaffold + LLM-generated domain logic.
# The template provides ONLY safety utilities. The LLM generates COMPLETE
# DataGenerator specifications directly — no hidden base_generator abstraction.
#
# Template provides (safety utilities):
#   - get_table_col_types(): schema discovery from catalog
#   - spark_type_for(): DDL → PySpark type mapping
#   - extract_max_length(): VARCHAR(N) constraint detection
#   - enforce_varchar_limits(): truncation safety net before write
#   - verify_before_write(): pre-write validation gate (PK unique, FK diverse, no generic values)
#   - _safe_withColumn patch: BooleanType/TimestampType dbldatagen bug workarounds
#
# LLM generates (domain intelligence — one cell per table):
#   - DOMAIN_COLS dict: categorical columns with explicit values + weights
#   - MUST call validate_domain_cols() BEFORE generate_table() (DETERMINISM GATE)
#   - MUST call validate_fk_replacements() for FK columns (DETERMINISM GATE)
#   - Complete DataGenerator: domain cols first, bulk cols via type-dispatch loop
#   - FK replacement: sampled parent keys after build()
#   - Business key uniqueness: sequential unique identifiers
#
# DETERMINISM CONTRACT:
#   The LLM MUST use validate_domain_cols() before every generate_table() call.
#   This function checks column names against DESCRIBE TABLE and RAISES if
#   any domain_col key doesn't match an actual column. This prevents the
#   silent fallback to garbage data that occurs when column names are wrong.
#
# PYTHON COMPATIBILITY (Python 3.11 — serverless compute):
#   DO NOT use backslashes inside f-string expressions.
#   ILLEGAL:  f"{', '.join(items)}"  — OK
#   ILLEGAL:  f"{'\n'.join(items)}"  — SYNTAX ERROR
#   ILLEGAL:  f"{val.replace('\n', '')}" — SYNTAX ERROR
#   FIX:      Use a variable: nl = '\n'; f"{nl.join(items)}"
#   FIX:      Or use .format(): "\n".join(items)
#   FIX:      Or assign first: joined = '\n'.join(items); f"{joined}"
#   This is a hard Python 3.11 syntax constraint. Violation = notebook crash.

